{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01ECeaXQuVvYxtMmX8yUvfH1","type":"tool_result","content":"{\"result\":\"{\\\"answer\\\":\\\"The encoding adopted for the serialized headers blob size field in the KIP-1271 proposal is a varint (variable-length integer), adopted in the KIP update of 2026-01-16, replacing the earlier fixed 2-byte length field design.\\\\n\\\\nSupporting facts in time order:\\\\n\\\\n- 2026-01-12T21:07:50.000000Z - The original design used a fixed 2-byte header length field, described as a hard limit of 64 KB of headers data (Alieh Saeedi's message: \\\\\\\"The maximum header length is fixed at 2 bytes, which corresponds to 64 KB of headers data (in byte[]). This is a hard limit\\\\\\\"). This is the earlier design that was later replaced. [mail:fddcc74d4df53f96c1a36885]\\\\n- 2026-01-15T14:58:26.000000Z - Alieh Saeedi proposed \\\\\\\"using a varint for the header length to remain flexible and space-efficient\\\\\\\" as an alternative to a fixed-size field. This is a discussion step before the adoption. [mail:96a17a05557028a239b6ad40]\\\\n- 2026-01-16 - The KIP was updated to introduce \\\\\\\"a varint header_size field ... to enable lazy deserialization when scanning large ranges.\\\\\\\" This is the adoption of the varint encoding. [mail:bd104003033b64b1b66392f7] [mail:cc6691acddb4132e5b46a141]\\\\n\\\\nWhat could not be determined: The github records (PRs #21297 and #21345) contained no comment, review comment, or commit text stating the encoding or mentioning the fixed 2-byte design, so they do not contribute to this answer; the mail records provide the determination. No other gaps remain.\\\",\\\"error\\\":null,\\\"files\\\":[\\\"mail:fddcc74d4df53f96c1a36885\\\",\\\"mail:96a17a05557028a239b6ad40\\\",\\\"mail:bd104003033b64b1b66392f7\\\",\\\"mail:cc6691acddb4132e5b46a141\\\",\\\"github:api:apache/kafka:pr-21297-opened\\\",\\\"github:api:apache/kafka:pr-21345-opened\\\"],\\\"seconds\\\":71.345,\\\"truncated\\\":false,\\\"turns\\\":10,\\\"usage\\\":{\\\"cache_read_tokens\\\":null,\\\"cache_write_tokens\\\":null,\\\"cost_usd\\\":0.00434287,\\\"input_tokens\\\":99460,\\\"output_tokens\\\":12195}}\"}"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"8906c5c4-e15c-4470-8d8e-adf2290655f4","uuid":"5620eaea-516b-48cf-b7a8-365c76e296d1","timestamp":"2026-09-29T14:41:09.645Z","tool_use_result":{"content":"{\"result\":\"{\\\"answer\\\":\\\"The encoding adopted for the serialized headers blob size field in the KIP-1271 proposal is a varint (variable-length integer), adopted in the KIP update of 2026-01-16, replacing the earlier fixed 2-byte length field design.\\\\n\\\\nSupporting facts in time order:\\\\n\\\\n- 2026-01-12T21:07:50.000000Z - The original design used a fixed 2-byte header length field, described as a hard limit of 64 KB of headers data (Alieh Saeedi's message: \\\\\\\"The maximum header length is fixed at 2 bytes, which corresponds to 64 KB of headers data (in byte[]). This is a hard limit\\\\\\\"). This is the earlier design that was later replaced. [mail:fddcc74d4df53f96c1a36885]\\\\n- 2026-01-15T14:58:26.000000Z - Alieh Saeedi proposed \\\\\\\"using a varint for the header length to remain flexible and space-efficient\\\\\\\" as an alternative to a fixed-size field. This is a discussion step before the adoption. [mail:96a17a05557028a239b6ad40]\\\\n- 2026-01-16 - The KIP was updated to introduce \\\\\\\"a varint header_size field ... to enable lazy deserialization when scanning large ranges.\\\\\\\" This is the adoption of the varint encoding. [mail:bd104003033b64b1b66392f7] [mail:cc6691acddb4132e5b46a141]\\\\n\\\\nWhat could not be determined: The github records (PRs #21297 and #21345) contained no comment, review comment, or commit text stating the encoding or mentioning the fixed 2-byte design, so they do not contribute to this answer; the mail records provide the determination. No other gaps remain.\\\",\\\"error\\\":null,\\\"files\\\":[\\\"mail:fddcc74d4df53f96c1a3